\section{Introduction}

This report presents four distributed programs: two built on the MapReduce model and
two on gRPC. Section~2 implements distributed matrix multiplication. Section~3
implements the Homework~2 log-analytics problem as a batch MapReduce job, and
Section~4 implements the same analytics as a real-time streaming system, so that the
two paradigms can be compared on identical work. Section~5 implements a food ordering
service. Section~6 describes the execution environment and the measurement method;
Section~7 draws the comparisons together.

The emphasis throughout is on \emph{why} each design was chosen and what the
measurements reveal about it. All figures reported here were obtained on the RCE
cluster and are reproduced directly from the recorded result files.

\paragraph{Execution model for MapReduce.}
The assignment specifies Apache Hadoop~3.3.6 on YARN. This is not available on RCE:
the shared HDFS is read-only for a student account (its root is owned by
\texttt{hdfs:supergroup} with mode \texttt{drwxr-xr-x}) and no ResourceManager is
configured, so no YARN application can be submitted. The course has confirmed a known
issue with the Hadoop environment and that a Slurm-based script is acceptable in the
interim. Both MapReduce programs are therefore driven by shell scripts that reproduce
the Hadoop Streaming execution flow exactly:
\[
\text{split} \rightarrow \text{map (parallel)} \rightarrow \text{sort} \rightarrow
\text{combine} \rightarrow \text{sort} \rightarrow \text{reduce}
\]
The mapper, combiner and reducer are standalone executables communicating only through
standard input and standard output, which is precisely the Hadoop Streaming contract;
they would run unmodified under a working Hadoop installation.

\section{Distributed Matrix Multiplication}

\subsection{Problem and approach}
Given $A$ of dimension $m \times n$ and $B$ of dimension $n \times p$, compute
$C = A \times B$ by the \textbf{Row--Row method}, in which each row of $C$ is a
weighted sum of the rows of $B$:
\[
c_i \;=\; a_{i,1}B_{1,:} \;+\; a_{i,2}B_{2,:} \;+\; \cdots \;+\; a_{i,n}B_{n,:}
\]
This formulation is attractive for MapReduce because each row of $C$ depends on exactly
one row of $A$ and on all of $B$. If $B$ is replicated to every mapper, a mapper can
compute a complete contribution to $c_i$ from row $i$ alone, with no communication
between mappers. The row index is then a natural reduce key.

\subsection{Data distribution}
$A$ is stored row-by-row and split across mappers; $B$ is \textbf{replicated into the
local memory of every mapper} before processing begins. This asymmetry is deliberate:
$A$ is the large operand and is partitioned, while $B$ is broadcast so that no mapper
ever needs data held by another. Each input row of $A$ carries its own global row index,
so a mapper can label its output correctly without knowing which split it received.

\subsection{Key--value schema}
\begin{center}\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}llp{7.6cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Stage} & \textbf{Key} & \textbf{Value} \\
\midrule
Map output & row index $i$, zero-padded & partial vector $a_{ik}\cdot B_{k,:}$, one per $k$ \\
Combine    & row index $i$ & element-wise sum of that node's partial vectors for $i$ \\
Reduce out & --- & the completed row $c_i$, emitted in ascending $i$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Keys are zero-padded so that lexicographic sorting, which is what the shuffle performs,
agrees with numeric ordering. The combiner consumes and produces the same format as the
mapper, so the reducer cannot distinguish a run in which the combiner executed from one
in which it did not --- the property a combiner must satisfy. It is valid here because
vector addition is associative and commutative, so partial sums may be grouped
arbitrarily.

\subsection{Complexity}
A mapper emits $n$ partial vectors of length $p$ for each of its rows, so a split of
$m'$ rows produces $O(m'np)$ intermediate values. The combiner reduces this to $O(m'p)$
before the network shuffle --- a factor of $n$ --- which is the single most important
property of the design, since intermediate volume rather than input size governs the
cost. The reducer performs $O(mp)$ additions in total.

\subsection{Correctness}
Every result is checked against a direct triple-loop multiplication of the same
matrices. Nine configurations were executed distributed across four nodes, chosen to
cover the cases the problem statement identifies as significant: the two worked examples
from the assignment; the edge cases $m=1$ and $n=1$; inputs whose row count is and is
not divisible by the number of mappers; and skewed shapes in both directions. All nine
produced results identical to the reference computation.

\begin{center}\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lllc@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Configuration} & \textbf{$A$} & \textbf{$B$} & \textbf{$m$ divisible by 4} \\
\midrule
Assignment Example 1 & $3\times2$ & $2\times3$ & no \\
Assignment Example 2 & $4\times2$ & $2\times2$ & yes \\
Edge case, single row & $1\times3$ & $3\times2$ & no \\
Edge case, single column & $3\times1$ & $1\times3$ & no \\
Baseline & $100\times50$ & $50\times50$ & yes \\
Square & $1000\times100$ & $100\times1000$ & yes \\
Square, indivisible & $1001\times100$ & $100\times1000$ & no \\
Tall, $m \gg n$ & $5000\times10$ & $10\times10$ & yes \\
Wide, $n \gg m$ & $10\times500$ & $500\times10$ & no \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\subsection{Performance}
Four nodes, four map tasks. Times in seconds.

\begin{center}\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}llccrrrrr@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Case} & \textbf{$A$} & \textbf{$B$} & \textbf{div.} & \textbf{Map} & \textbf{Shuffle} & \textbf{Combine} & \textbf{Reduce} & \textbf{Total} \\
\midrule
@@sec1_tbl@@
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\textbf{Intermediate volume dominates, not input size.} The two square cases take
roughly $30$\,s while $2000\times50$ takes $1.7$\,s, although their inputs are of
comparable size. The difference follows directly from the $O(mnp)$ bound: with
$n=100$ and $p=1000$ each row produces 100 vectors of 1000 values, some 400\,MB of
intermediate text, and the map and shuffle stages carry that cost.

\textbf{Indivisible splits cost almost nothing.} 1000 rows and 1001 rows differ by
$0.85$\,s out of $30$\,s; the single extra row is handled by one node with no special
case in the code.

\textbf{The combiner is what keeps the global merge cheap.} Although the intermediate
data reaches hundreds of megabytes, the second sort takes $0.05$\,s, because each node
has already collapsed its partial vectors to one row each before anything crosses the
network. This is the $O(n)$ reduction of the complexity analysis, visible directly in
the timings.

\textbf{Shape determines utilisation.} The tall case distributes 5000 rows over four
nodes and finishes in $0.77$\,s; the wide case has only 10 rows to distribute, so most
nodes are idle and the run is limited by a single mapper regardless of cluster size.

\section{Batch Log Analytics with MapReduce}

\subsection{Problem}
The Homework~2 analytics over a server log: total, successful and failed request counts;
counts by status class; minimum, maximum and mean response time; total bytes
transferred; the busiest 60-second interval; and the Top-$K$ servers and endpoints.
Implemented in C++ as mapper, combiner and reducer executables.

\subsection{The property that makes the job decomposable}
Every quantity retained is a \textbf{count, a sum, a minimum or a maximum}. Each of
these is associative and commutative, so a partial result computed over any subset of
the input can be combined with any other, in any order, to give exactly the result that
would have been obtained from the whole. This is the precondition for splitting the work
at all, and it dictates two design decisions:

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.3em,topsep=2pt,itemsep=2pt]
\item \textbf{Averages are never stored.} A mean is not mergeable --- the mean of two
      means is not the mean of the union unless the counts are equal. The sum and the
      count are stored instead, and the division is performed once, when the final
      answer is printed.
\item \textbf{Response times are stored as integers}, in units of $10^{-6}$\,ms. Floating
      point addition is not associative, so a floating-point total would depend on the
      order in which mappers happened to be merged, and that order varies between runs.
      Integers make the result reproducible and exactly equal to the sequential program's.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{In-mapper combining}
A mapper does not emit one record per input line. It accumulates its entire split in
memory and emits one pair per \emph{distinct key} it observed:

\begin{center}\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}llp{8.2cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Key} & \textbf{Meaning} & \textbf{Value} \\
\midrule
\texttt{K} & the Top-$K$ size & taken from the dataset header \\
\texttt{G} & split-wide totals & counts, byte total, time sum, minimum, maximum \\
\texttt{S:$id$} & one server & request count, summed response time \\
\texttt{E:$id$} & one endpoint & request count, summed bytes \\
\texttt{I:$t$} & one 60-second interval & request count \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

The consequence is that intermediate data is bounded by the \emph{key space} --- the
number of distinct servers, endpoints and intervals --- and not by the number of
records. On the ten-million-record dataset the shuffle is
\textbf{@@shuffle_ratio@@$\times$ smaller than the input}. Since the sort is the
expensive part of a MapReduce job, this is the decision that determines the program's
performance.

\subsection{Why a single reducer}
Most of the analytics could be computed by any number of reducers and combined
afterwards. The Top-$K$ lists could not: identifying the ten busiest servers requires
the totals of \emph{all} servers, and a server ranked eleventh on every reducer may
still rank first overall. A single reducer therefore observes the complete key space.
This is affordable only because in-mapper combining has already reduced the data it must
read to a few megabytes.

\subsection{Correctness}
The requirement is stricter than numerical agreement: the output must match the
Homework~2 sequential program byte for byte. Thirty-four checks were performed, covering
the worked example; five edge cases (empty input, a single record, ties in the Top-$K$
ordering, $K=0$, and response times as small as $10^{-6}$\,ms); and the same input
divided into 1, 2, 4 and 8 splits, since a correct decomposition must give an identical
answer however the input is divided. All thirty-four matched, as did all
@@q1_ok@@ runs of the performance sweep described below.

\subsection{Performance}
Map tasks placed one per node across four nodes. Times in seconds.

\begin{center}\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrrrr@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Map tasks} & \textbf{100k records} & \textbf{1M records} & \textbf{10M records} & \textbf{10M: map stage} & \textbf{Speedup} \\
\midrule
@@s2q1_tbl@@
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\textbf{The map stage is the computation.} It accounts for 96\% of the single-task run.
Measured separately on a compute node, reading the 397\,MB input with \texttt{cat} takes
$0.26$\,s while the mapper takes $13.26$\,s, of which $13.16$\,s is user CPU time. The
work is parsing ten million lines of text, not transferring them, and the program is
therefore compute-bound and scales with available cores.

\textbf{Parallelism is limited by physical cores, not by the algorithm.} Speedup tracks
the map stage closely up to four tasks (@@large_speedup@@$\times$ on four nodes). At
eight tasks the map stage does not improve at all, because the scheduler places the
second task on some nodes onto a sibling hyperthread of a core already in use; two
processes sharing one physical core take twice as long each. The additional tasks still
enlarge the sort and combine stages, so the total time rises slightly. This is a
property of the allocation, not of the decomposition.

\textbf{The serial tail is present but small.} The final gather, sort and reduce do not
shrink as mappers are added; they grow from $0.22$\,s to $0.28$\,s, because more mappers
emit more partial rows for the same keys. This is the serial fraction of Amdahl's law,
and at under 6\% of the four-task run it is not the binding constraint here.

\textbf{Small inputs should not be distributed.} At 100k records the four-task run is
barely faster than the single-task run and the eight-task run is clearly slower: process
startup across nodes exceeds the work being parallelised. MapReduce carries a fixed
overhead that is only amortised from roughly a million records upward.

\subsection{Comparison with MPI}
The same analytics were implemented with MPI, splitting the input by byte range and
combining with \texttt{MPI\_Reduce} (\texttt{MPI\_SUM} for counts and sums,
\texttt{MPI\_MIN} and \texttt{MPI\_MAX} for the extremes). Both systems were given equal
core counts. MPI runs all ranks on one node, since Open MPI cannot launch across nodes
in this environment; MapReduce distributes its tasks over four. All @@mpi_ok@@ MPI runs
reproduced the sequential output.

\begin{center}\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrrrr@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Processes} & \textbf{MPI} & \textbf{MapReduce} & \textbf{Ratio} & \textbf{MPI speedup} & \textbf{MapReduce speedup} \\
\midrule
@@mpi_tbl@@
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\textbf{MPI is between 1.4 and 2.4 times faster at every size}, and the ratio is stable
at about $2.1\times$ on the largest dataset. This is the expected outcome: MPI performs
no shuffle, no sort, no serialisation to text and no process creation per stage. It
holds its state in memory and reduces once at the end.

\textbf{The two scale almost identically} --- @@mpi_speedup@@$\times$ and
@@large_speedup@@$\times$ respectively at four processes --- so the parallel
decomposition is equally effective in both. The difference is entirely in the constant
factor, which makes it worth identifying where that constant comes from.

\subsection{Where the constant factor comes from}
The two programs differ in how they represent state. Measured on the ten-million-record
dataset:

\begin{center}\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}llrr@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Program} & \textbf{State representation} & \textbf{Peak memory} & \textbf{Time} \\
\midrule
Sequential (HW2) & two passes; all records buffered; dense arrays & 395\,MB & 4.65\,s \\
MPI, one rank & as above, with a byte-range split & 403\,MB & 6.31\,s \\
Mapper (this work) & one streaming pass; sparse maps & \textbf{10.1\,MB} & 13.00\,s \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

The Homework~2 design pre-scans the input to determine the range of server and endpoint
identifiers, allocates dense arrays, and then indexes them directly: an array subscript
per record, which is as fast as this computation can be. The cost is that it must hold
all ten million records in memory first.

The mapper cannot do this. A mapper sees only its own split and therefore cannot know
the global identifier ranges in advance, so it counts in a single streaming pass into
sparse associative containers, discarding each record once counted. The price is a tree
lookup per record rather than an array subscript.

The result is a \textbf{39$\times$ reduction in memory for a factor of about $2.8$ in
speed}. This is not a defect in either program but a genuine engineering trade, and the
memory property is what permits the MapReduce decomposition in the first place: a design
requiring 395\,MB per task and a prior pass over the whole input cannot be split across
mappers that each see a fraction of the data. A single-pass MPI implementation using
sparse state would combine both advantages and would be expected to outperform
everything measured here.

\section{Real-Time Log Analytics with gRPC}

\subsection{Problem and architecture}
The same analytics, but with the data treated as a continuous stream rather than a
complete batch, and with the current results queryable at any time during ingestion.

\begin{center}\small
\begin{verbatim}
 stream_client --StreamRecords--> +-- Coordinator --+ --Process()--> Worker 0
 (replays the dataset)            |  router         | --Process()--> Worker 1
                                  |  bounded queues | --Process()--> Worker 2
 dashboard  --WatchAnalytics--->  |  adds workers up| <--GetStats()-- (each counts
 query_client --GetAnalytics--->  +-----------------+                 into its own)
\end{verbatim}
\end{center}

The coordinator performs three roles: it accepts record streams, routes each batch to a
worker, and answers queries by combining the workers' partial results. Workers count
records into their own state and never communicate with one another or with clients.
The mergeability property established in Section~3 is what makes this possible: the
global answer is the combination of the workers' states, computed on demand.

\subsection{Interface design}
\begin{center}\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}llp{8.1cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{RPC} & \textbf{Kind} & \textbf{Rationale} \\
\midrule
\texttt{StreamRecords} & client streaming & One long-lived call carries an entire
stream: a header, then any number of batches. Using one call per stream rather than per
batch avoids repeated connection and header costs, and gRPC's flow control then supplies
backpressure without additional mechanism. \\
\texttt{GetAnalytics} & unary & The current analytics, answered while ingestion
continues. \\
\texttt{WatchAnalytics} & server streaming & A snapshot pushed at a fixed interval, so
that $N$ dashboards cost one combination per refresh rather than $N$. \\
\texttt{Worker.Process} & client streaming & All batches for a worker travel on a single
persistent call. \\
\texttt{Worker.GetStats} & unary & Returns the worker's complete running totals. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\textbf{Idempotent aggregation.} Each worker maintains its own running totals and always
returns all of them; the coordinator \emph{replaces} that worker's contribution rather
than accumulating it. Repeating a request is therefore harmless, which removes the need
for sequence numbers, acknowledgements or duplicate detection. An earlier version
transmitted numbered deltas that the coordinator had to acknowledge, with recovery rules
for lost replies; replacing rather than accumulating made that protocol unnecessary and
removed a substantial amount of code.

\textbf{Columnar batches.} A batch is encoded as one packed array per field rather than
as repeated sub-messages, so a 1000-record batch is seven packed arrays instead of a
thousand small messages. Protocol Buffers decodes packed scalars in native code, and the
worker iterates the columns directly.

\subsection{Concurrency and flow control}
Each worker has a \textbf{bounded} queue. When a worker falls behind, its queue fills,
the enqueue operation blocks, the ingestion handler stops reading from the stream, and
gRPC's flow control propagates the pause to the client. Memory therefore remains bounded
regardless of how fast the source sends. A worker's state is guarded by a single lock
held for a whole batch, so a concurrent query can never observe a partially applied
batch.

\subsection{Correctness}
Once the stream has been fully processed, the analytics must equal the sequential
program's output exactly. Seventy-six checks were performed: one to three workers
combined with three distribution strategies and several batch sizes; two sources
streaming concurrently; queries issued while ingestion was still in progress; and $K$
supplied at query time rather than taken from the dataset header. All matched, as did
all @@q2_ok@@ runs of the performance sweep.

\subsection{Performance}
One million records, one process per node, coordinator, workers and clients all on
separate machines.

\begin{minipage}[t]{0.46\textwidth}\small
\textbf{Effect of worker count}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrr@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Workers} & \textbf{records/s} & \textbf{Speedup} \\ \midrule
@@work_tbl@@
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
\end{minipage}\hfill
\begin{minipage}[t]{0.5\textwidth}\small
\textbf{Effect of distribution strategy}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrr@{}}
