\vspace{0.6em}
\textbf{Effect of message granularity}
\begin{center}\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}rrrr@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Records per message} & \textbf{Messages per million} & \textbf{records/s} & \textbf{Relative} \\ \midrule
@@batch_tbl@@
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\textbf{Effect of concurrent queries.} Each client issues queries in a closed loop with
no pause between them, which is the most demanding pattern possible.
\begin{center}\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrrrr@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Query clients} & \textbf{Ingest (rec/s)} & \textbf{Change} & \textbf{Queries/s} & \textbf{p50 (ms)} & \textbf{p99 (ms)} \\ \midrule
@@query_tbl@@
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\textbf{Scaling is near linear to four workers} (@@q2_speedup@@$\times$), because workers
share no state and require no coordination. The shortfall from a perfect $4\times$ is
attributable to the coordinator, through which every record must pass.

\textbf{Message granularity is the dominant parameter}, worth @@batch_gain@@$\times$
between one record per message and one thousand. Up to a batch of 100 the throughput
rises almost exactly tenfold for each tenfold increase in batch size, which identifies
the cost as \emph{per message} rather than per record: framing, a handler iteration, a
queue transfer and a forwarding call are each paid once per message irrespective of its
contents. Beyond 1000 the curve flattens, since per-message overhead has been amortised
and per-record work dominates. A batch of 1000 is therefore the appropriate default: it
lies at the start of the plateau, and at these rates a batch waits about one millisecond
to fill, so the analytics remain effectively current.

\textbf{Partitioning by key is expensive and unnecessary here.} The
\texttt{hash\_server} strategy is roughly fourteen times slower than round robin because
it is the only strategy that examines every \emph{record} rather than every
\emph{batch}: the coordinator must disassemble each batch and re-encode one batch per
worker, which is $O(\text{records})$ work in the single process that is already the
bottleneck. Key affinity would be required if a worker had to hold all state for a given
key, but every analytic here is mergeable, so it is not.

\textbf{Load-aware routing did not pay off.} \texttt{least\_loaded} is slower than round
robin, contrary to its intent: it inspects every worker queue for every batch, and since
the workers are near-identical it pays that cost without ever finding a meaningfully
shorter queue. It would be justified only with heterogeneous or contended workers.

\textbf{Queries are served throughout ingestion, and they are not free.} A single
closed-loop client reduces ingest throughput by 21\%, four by 73\% and sixteen by 93\%,
while the rate of answers saturates near 150 per second. Each query requires the
coordinator to contact every worker and combine their states, and this occurs in the
same single-threaded process that routes records. Beyond roughly four concurrent
closed-loop clients the coordinator is saturated and further clients merely queue.
\textbf{This is the principal limitation of the architecture.} Two remedies follow
directly from the diagnosis: partition the coordinator so that routing and query service
are not serialised through one process, or answer queries from a snapshot refreshed on a
timer, trading a bounded amount of staleness for throughput.

\section{Food Ordering System with gRPC}

\subsection{Structure}
A central server maintains a fixed catalogue of restaurants and the state of all orders.
Customer clients and restaurant clients are separate processes, run on separate nodes,
and interact only through the server.

The service exposes \texttt{ListRestaurants}, \texttt{PlaceOrder},
\texttt{GetOrderStatus}, \texttt{UpdateOrderStatus} and \texttt{SubscribeToOrderUpdates},
together with a helper, \texttt{ListRestaurantOrders}, which allows a restaurant client
to retrieve its own pending orders without maintaining client-side state.
\texttt{SubscribeToOrderUpdates} is a server-streaming call: a customer subscribes to an
order and the server pushes each status change as it occurs.

\subsection{Order lifecycle}
An order progresses through
\texttt{PLACED} $\rightarrow$ \texttt{ACCEPTED} $\rightarrow$ \texttt{PREPARING}
$\rightarrow$ \texttt{READY}, and may instead be cancelled from \texttt{PLACED}.
The permitted transitions are held in an explicit table and any transition absent from
it is refused, so an order cannot move backwards and cannot be cancelled once a
restaurant has accepted it. Encoding the state machine as data rather than as scattered
conditionals means the validity rule is stated in one place.

\subsection{Concurrency and error handling}
All shared state is protected by a single lock. Notifications to subscribers are
dispatched \emph{outside} that lock, via a per-subscriber queue, so that a slow or
unresponsive subscriber cannot delay other requests.

Errors are reported with the gRPC status code appropriate to each condition rather than
being silently ignored: \texttt{NOT\_FOUND} for an unknown restaurant, an unavailable
item or an unknown order; \texttt{PERMISSION\_DENIED} when a restaurant attempts to
modify an order belonging to another; and \texttt{FAILED\_PRECONDITION} for a
transition the state machine disallows, including cancellation of an order already
accepted.

\subsection{Verification}
The system was exercised with the server on one compute node and clients driven from two
others. The sequence demonstrated was: retrieval of both restaurant menus; placement of
an order for one Margherita Pizza and two Garlic Breads, for which the server computed
the expected total of 550 and the initial status \texttt{PLACED}; the restaurant
advancing the order through \texttt{ACCEPTED}, \texttt{PREPARING} and \texttt{READY},
with each transition delivered to the subscribed customer over the stream in order; a
second customer placing a concurrent order and receiving a distinct identifier; and each
of the six error conditions above returning its intended status code.

\subsection{Known limitation}
\texttt{SubscribeToOrderUpdates} waits on its queue without a timeout while occupying a
thread from a pool of ten. Ten customers tracking orders simultaneously --- ordinary use
--- exhaust the pool, after which the server ceases to answer any request. Subscribing to
an order that has already reached \texttt{READY} blocks indefinitely for the same reason,
as no further update will ever be produced. Both follow from the same cause and are
addressed by waiting on the queue with a timeout while re-checking whether the client
remains connected, by transmitting the current status upon subscription, and by
enlarging the thread pool.

\section{Execution Environment and Method}

Measurements were taken on the RCE cluster. Compute nodes are dual Intel Xeon Gold 5317
processors, providing 24 physical cores and 48 logical processors with hyperthread
siblings at a stride of 24, with 502\,GB of memory and a shared NFS home directory.
Jobs are allocated by Slurm. Servers and clients are always placed on distinct compute
nodes, never on the login node, and always addressed by node name rather than by
\texttt{localhost}.

\paragraph{A methodological point affecting all timings.}
Requesting \texttt{-{}-nodes=N -{}-ntasks-per-node=1} without also requesting
\texttt{-{}-cpus-per-task} yields \emph{one physical core per node}. The reported
processor count of two is misleading, as those are the two hyperthreads of a single
core: under such an allocation two mapper processes on one node require $2.49$\,s where
one requires $1.27$\,s, and \texttt{mpirun -np 8} confines eight ranks to a single core.
Every measurement in this report requests \texttt{-{}-cpus-per-task} explicitly, so that
$N$ tasks and $N$ MPI ranks each receive $N$ physical cores. Without this precaution the
MPI program appears not to scale at all, which is an artefact of the allocation rather
than a property of the program.

\paragraph{Reproducibility.} Datasets are produced by a generator with fixed seeds.
Every performance run records its own correctness verdict alongside its timings, and
runs that did not reproduce the reference output would be excluded from the analysis;
no run in the reported sweeps failed to do so. The figures in this report are generated
directly from the recorded result files.

\section{Conclusions}

\textbf{The same problem in two paradigms.} Sections~3 and~4 implement identical
analytics as a batch job and as a streaming system. The batch implementation is simpler
and produces its answer only at completion; the streaming implementation maintains the
answer continuously and serves queries during ingestion, at the cost of a coordinator
through which all data must pass. Neither is uniformly preferable: the choice follows
from whether the answer is needed during ingestion or only after it.

\textbf{State representation mattered more than the framework.} The clearest result of
the MPI comparison is that the difference in speed is attributable to dense versus sparse
state, not to MPI versus MapReduce. The same decision that makes our implementation
$2.8\times$ slower also makes it use $39\times$ less memory, and that reduction is what
permits the work to be divided among mappers that each observe only part of the input.

\textbf{Message granularity dominated the streaming system.} Varying the batch size
changed throughput by a factor of @@batch_gain@@, far exceeding the effect of the worker
count or the routing strategy. Where a cost is incurred per message rather than per
record, aggregation into larger messages is the most effective optimisation available.

\textbf{Each system's limit was identified and attributed.} The batch pipeline is
bounded by parsing throughput and by the physical cores available; the streaming system
is bounded by its single coordinator under concurrent query load; the ordering service
is bounded by the thread pool consumed by long-lived subscriptions. In each case the
constraint was located by measurement rather than inferred, and the corresponding remedy
follows from the diagnosis.

